# MEASUREMENT PROBE — can the numeral comparison be reduced STRUCTURALLY, and
# is the literal's missing defining equation what blocks it?
# Worktree-only, never to be landed.
#
# `git apply scripts/nat-accel-withhold.diff` onto a scratch worktree, then
#
#   cargo build --release --features tg,stepcount --bin nanoda_bin
#   ./target/release/nanoda_bin cfg.json                  # baseline
#   NANODA_NAT_CMP_MIN=13 …    # withhold GMP beq/ble at operands < 13
#   NANODA_IOTA_RULED=1 …      # let `Iota` count as `reduct::fully_ruled`
#   (and the two together, and with NANODA_NAT_RULED=1)
#
# `cfg.json` is `good/init-prelude.ndjson`, `nat_extension` and
# `string_extension` on, `num_threads: 1`, `unsafe_permit_all_axioms`.
#
# WHAT IT ANSWERS. `try_reduce_nat` short-circuits `Nat.ble` before δ, so the
# δ+ι route is entered only if the accelerator stands aside. Withholding it is
# affordable — **+0.55 % instructions**, `perf stat -e instructions`, despite
# 1.84x the reduction steps — and it is also worthless: every combination is
# WORSE than the baseline (+148 / +512 / +660 / +698 gaps), and the 466
# `Bool.rec (Nat.ble ⟨a⟩ ⟨b⟩)` sites are *unmoved*, because `reduce_rec` still
# presents the unreduced major. Above all, only **10** ι asks arrive with a nat
# literal as the major premise, so a defining equation per literal —
# `⊢ lit_n = app(⟦Nat.succ⟧, lit_{n−1})` — would serve 10 sites of 466.
#
# It also carries the `Admit::NatRed` census of `scripts/nat-red-census.diff`,
# so the two halves can be read off one run.
#
# Ledger byte-identical with the probe applied and no variable set:
# 13 254 gaps, 2 432 escapes, 162 483 derived.
